% ============================================================================
% circle-parts — the parts of a circle and the defining phrase the source
% states for each one (adj-facts-stdlib, GEOMETRY).
% ============================================================================
% A circle is not one undifferentiated ring: it is described by a small family
% of named PARTS and MEASURES, and a mathematics reference pins each one to the
% ONE phrase that defines it. "The radius runs from the center to the perimeter;
% the diameter is the maximum distance across; the circumference is the
% perimeter; a chord is a segment whose ends lie on the circle." Before you can
% reason about a circle — measure it, draw it, or teach it — you must first know
% WHICH part you mean and what defines it. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? circle_part(radius, $D)      % center_to_perimeter
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `circle_part(part, description)` carrying the table's citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% defining phrase WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and
% abstains on a word that is not one of these circle parts (it never invents a
% description).
%
% Each circle part and the defining phrase the source states for it, as a
% little truth table:
%
%     circle part     defining phrase (atom)      a beginner's cue
%     -----------     ----------------------      -----------------------------
%     radius          center_to_perimeter         center out to the edge
%     diameter        maximum_distance_across      the longest straight cut
%     circumference   perimeter                    the length once around
%     chord           ends_on_circle              a segment both ends on the ring
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a description and recall the part:
%
%     ? circle_part($Part, perimeter)   % circumference
%
% A word that is NOT one of these grounded circle parts — `vertex`, `angle`,
% `side` — is deliberately NOT a row, so a circle-part recall ABSTAINS on it
% rather than inventing a description. (A vertex is a corner of a POLYGON; a
% circle has none.) That honest-abstention property is what makes the table safe
% to build a reasoner on: it answers exactly the part→description pairs it can
% ground, and only those.
%
% The `description` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase atom, chosen to be
% words the source EXPLICITLY uses for that part — never words the source does
% not support. Where the source's phrase is more than one word (the radius runs
% "from the center of a circle to its perimeter"), the atom joins the source's
% own words with underscores, so every value stays literally checkable against
% the quoted span.
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every part→description pair
% below was read out of a fetched Wolfram MathWorld page this session, and any
% pair whose description could not be verified there was dropped). MathWorld
% (mathworld.wolfram.com) is a primary mathematics reference, so every row is
% `trust authoritative`. Each pair, with the verbatim span that states it:
%
%   radius → center_to_perimeter
%     "The distance from the center of a circle to its perimeter, or from the
%      center of a sphere to its surface."
%     https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Radius.html
%
%   diameter → maximum_distance_across
%     "The diameter of a circle is the distance from a point on the circle to a
%      point pi radians away, and is the maximum distance from one point on a
%      circle to another."
%     https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Diameter.html
%
%   circumference → perimeter
%     "In the work, the term "circumference" is used to mean the perimeter of a
%      circle."
%     https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Circumference.html
%
%   chord → ends_on_circle
%     "The term is often used to describe a line segment whose ends lie on a
%      circle."
%     https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Chord.html
%
% An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope, so the `source`/`locator`/
% `trust` below hold the single cleanest span: the MathWorld statement that
% fixes the first row (radius → center_to_perimeter). Every OTHER row's verbatim
% span (and its own MathWorld URL) is listed above, so each description remains
% independently checkable. `trust authoritative` — a primary mathematics
% reference. (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table circle_part {
    columns part, description

    row (radius, center_to_perimeter)         % "from the center of a circle to its perimeter"
    row (diameter, maximum_distance_across)    % "the maximum distance from one point on a circle to another"
    row (circumference, perimeter)             % '"circumference" is used to mean the perimeter of a circle'
    row (chord, ends_on_circle)               % "a line segment whose ends lie on a circle"

    source "The distance from the center of a circle to its perimeter, or from the center of a sphere to its surface."
    locator "https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Radius.html"
    trust authoritative
}
